# Strahd's attention (F9, data/strahd/attention.json): what he does once he has noticed the party. A face at the
# window at a night's rest once he is watching; a letter of grievances once they are marked; his spawn and wolves at
# their camp once they are hunted (data/strahd/visits.json). On the roads (data/random_encounters): his eyes once he
# is watching, his wolves at night once they are marked.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- At the window (watched)

~ window
Narrator: Somewhere past midnight the one on watch hears a soft knock at the shutters. Just one, polite as a guest.
Narrator: When they look, a bat hangs from the eaves, upside down and perfectly still, watching the room. It stays until they move toward it, then drops away into the dark without a sound.
if guest:ireena
interject guest:ireena: He used to send them to my window too. One every night. Father thought it was a kind of joke.
endif
interject tag:nervous: That wasn't a bat. Or it was, but it was also somebody.
-> END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- A letter of grievance (marked)

~ grievance
Narrator: A raven lands on the nearest post with a scroll tied to its leg in black ribbon. It waits, head cocked, for someone to take it, then flies off toward the mountains.
Strahd: My guests. I have kept a list. It is a habit of the old, and of the patient.
if flag.gulthias_tree_burned
Strahd: You burned my tree on Yester Hill. It was older than your nations. I do not expect you to understand what that means; I only want you to know that I do.
endif
if item:sunsword
Strahd: You carry my brother's sword. It did not save him either.
endif
if item:holy_symbol_of_ravenkind
Strahd: You wear the Morninglord's trinket where I can see it. I have seen it before. I watched its last bearer's light go out.
endif
if flag.lysaga_dead
Strahd: Baba Lysaga is dead. She held me when I was born. Whatever else she was, that was hers to keep, and you took it.
endif
if flag.kiril_defeated
Strahd: My wolves have a new leader. They will want to make a good impression.
endif
if flag.strahd_window and flag.strahd_window != "taken"
Strahd: You stood between Tatyana and me at her window. I remember every face that has done that. There have not been many, and they did not stay long.
endif
if flag.strahd_invitation == "declined"
Strahd: You have refused my carriage. I am told it was a fine evening for a drive.
endif
Strahd: I have been a generous host. I can be other things. Consider this the last of my courtesy, and consider it carefully.
interject tag:fierce: Good. I was getting tired of the courtesy.
interject tag:pious: He's afraid. A man who isn't afraid doesn't write lists.
-> END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The hunt (hunted)

~ hunt
Narrator: The fire gutters in no wind. Out past the light, the wolves stop howling all at once, and in the silence something laughs: not him, but something that learned how from him.
Narrator: Pale shapes come out of the trees on all fours, and the wolves come with them.
-> END

~ hunt_after
Narrator: The last of them goes still. Far up the valley a single wolf howls, and is not answered. Nobody sleeps much after that.
-> END

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- His eyes on the roads (watched)

~ eyes_bats
Narrator: A swarm of bats crosses the road ahead of you, turns back, and crosses again, low enough to stir your hair. Then it does it a third time, slower, as if counting.
interject tag:nervous: They're counting us. Don't let them count us.
-> END

~ eyes_wolves
Narrator: For a mile a grey wolf keeps pace with you just inside the treeline, never closer, never farther, never looking anywhere but at you. When the road bends, it is simply gone.
-> END

~ eyes_raven
Narrator: A black bird sits on a fence post and watches you pass. It is not one of the Keepers' ravens; those look away when you meet their eyes. This one doesn't.
-> END

~ eyes_vistana
Narrator: A Vistani wagon pulls aside to let you by, and the driver tips his hat, and asks where you are bound, and where you slept, and whether the lady with you is well, and how many you are, exactly.
interject tag:blunt: That's a lot of questions for a man with a horse to see to.
Narrator: He laughs, and drives on, and you notice he turns back the way you came.
-> END
